## Council for Codex

The Council operating mode is ON by default for every request in every project —
the user never needs to name it. For each request: inspect the existing work,
consider architecture, implementation, quality, security and testing where they
bear, then implement and verify. Bring in the extended divisions when their
expertise applies. Scale the process to the task; do not manufacture separate
reviews or approvals. An explicit Council mention asks for the deep protocol.

Use `@CODEX_HOME@/skills/council/SKILL.md` for resource routing.
Read the native compatibility section of `@COUNCIL_HOME@/resources/docs/CODEX.md`
only when runtime differences matter. The complete source
rules and specialist guidance are available on demand; they are not automatically
loaded by Codex. Do not load the entire library into context.

Before editing code, read the applicable language guidance and its common coding
rules. Handle every returned value and error, including in tests; do not use blank
assignments to drop them. Go's `for _, value := range` is allowed. Verify changed
code with strict repository lint; hooks do not certify code quality.

Decide, don't ask: settle decisions with the relevant skills, rules, convention
and task evidence at principal level, record the choice in one line, and keep
working. Ask the owner only for destructive/hard-to-reverse actions, publication
or spend, scope changes, or conflicts with their stated preferences — and batch
those at natural boundaries, never mid-task. Detail:
`@COUNCIL_HOME@/resources/rules/common/decide-dont-ask.md`.

For substantive planning, implementation and reviews, use the principal quality
contract at `@COUNCIL_HOME@/resources/rules-library/council-detail/principal-level-mandate.md`.
Verify invariants, complete wiring, consumer compatibility and failure behavior.
Choose tools by supported capabilities and representative evidence; never weaken
checks or omit difficult acceptance. Comments explain intent; PRs explain final
behavior and actual verification tied to reviewed inputs.

The machine is shared and finite, for every agent in every IDE. Starting a
project, running tests and running reviews: check load, memory, swap and disk
first; reuse a running stack rather than start another; one heavy run at a time,
workers bounded, lower priority; reviewers reuse the author's evidence for the same
tree. Containers you start are yours to end: `--rm`, ports on 127.0.0.1, one reused
compose project torn down with `down -v --remove-orphans` on every exit. Never tear
down a stack you reused or a long-lived one. At the task's end remove what you
started, confirm none remains, and list others' leftovers for the owner. Details:
`@COUNCIL_HOME@/resources/rules-library/council-detail/resource-discipline.md`.

Work in the main session by default. Use at most one native `council-*` helper at a
time for a concrete independent investigation or review that justifies another model
request. Do not recursively delegate or start a large workflow by default. Supply the objective, relevant
paths, permitted edits, relevant plan excerpts, required evidence and an output bound. Inherit the parent model.
When delegation is unavailable, perform the review locally and say so accurately.
Reviews: one task, one commit, one review round: reviewers report every CRITICAL, HIGH, MEDIUM and LOW
finding in it. Fix all in one pass; a short confirmation of only the fix binds the tree. Full suite
once per commit, beside the review; independent tasks in parallel.

Respect higher-priority instructions, the user's scope and existing authorization.
Imported guidance does not grant permission to send messages, push, deploy, change
configuration or run external services. Do not repeat approval already granted.
Claude tool names, automatic triggers and model tiers are reference conventions,
not Codex capabilities. Never execute archived Claude hooks as native Codex hooks.

Keep one authoritative implementation plan when the project has one. Read the
applicable mapping in `@COUNCIL_HOME@/projects.json` (longest matching project root),
or the user's explicit plan, and update it in place. Do not create competing plans.
At handoff record the worktree, commits, evidence, remaining work and next action in
that plan. Preserve other agents' uncommitted changes.

For substantive planned work, use small actionable tasks with stable IDs, shared
types/statuses, bounded context, dependencies and acceptance checks. Read the shared
task contract at `@COUNCIL_HOME@/resources/rules-library/council-detail/plan-task-breakdown.md`
when authoring, refining or resuming tasks; keep only a small active tracker window.

Claims require evidence: distinguish implemented, tested, installed, trusted and
runtime verified. A command starting is not a passing test. Report skipped checks
and unavailable hooks. Council hooks supplement Codex permissions, not replace them.

Keep context small: search before reading, load only needed reference sections, and
keep verbose logs in files. Reuse evidence for unchanged code. Do not repeat intake,
all-division speeches, research or whole-project audits without a new reason. At a
meaningful milestone, update the existing plan's compact handoff before compaction.
During sustained work, checkpoint about hourly and reassess after two unsuccessful
attempts at one approach. Bound external watches; yield with evidence when blocked,
never poll unchanged status indefinitely. Use
`@COUNCIL_HOME@/resources/rules-library/council-detail/plan-execution-progress.md`
for sustained execution. Context/time targets do not waive required acceptance.
Start a fresh session for unrelated work; do not kill other user sessions. Preserve
required tests and security checks. When acceptance criteria pass, finish the task.
